diff --git a/minigames/junk_platform/scripts/junk_platform_compactor.gd b/minigames/junk_platform/scripts/junk_platform_compactor.gd
--- a/minigames/junk_platform/scripts/junk_platform_compactor.gd
+++ b/minigames/junk_platform/scripts/junk_platform_compactor.gd
@@ -20,9 +20,69 @@ func slam():
 	cam_shaker.force_stop_shake()
 	cam_shaker.play_shake()
 
+# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+# [MP8] 🩸 T49 — compactor kills must be decided by the server (2026-08-27, player report + reproduced with 8 local instances)
+# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+#
+# **Symptom** — late in an 8-player match **the match never ends**. Everyone is gone, junk keeps falling, the frame rate keeps collapsing, and force-quitting is the only way out.
+# In the diagnostic log all 4 "alive" players sat at `(0, -999, 0)` with `active=false`
+# — which is exactly what `kill_rpc()` does. **The players were killed, but the server never updated its books.**
+#
+# ---- Why these three vanilla lines are a fuse ----
+#
+#     body.kill_rpc.rpc()                                    # RPC, runs on every peer
+#     body.emit_died.emit(body.player_presence.network_id)   # local signal, only fires on this machine
+#
+# `kill_rpc` is an RPC, while `emit_died` is a **plain signal**. And the listener of `emit_died`
+# (`junk_platform.gd::_on_player_died`, which removes the player from `active_players`) is connected in
+# `spawn_players()`, which **only runs on the server** (`all_players_loaded()` returns immediately on clients).
+#
+# So whenever **this code runs on a client**, `kill_rpc` kills the player on every peer, while `emit_died` on the client
+# fires into thin air — the server never learns this player died, `active_players` never shrinks,
+# and `check_game_end()` never sees `<= 1`. **This match has no timeout fallback, so this is a permanent hang.**
+#
+# ---- Why 1.2 didn't hit it and 1.3 did (this is the key point, don't read it as a "vanilla bug") ----
+#
+# The compactor carries two roles at once, **visuals** and **hit detection**.
+# Once `visuals_base` rotates, the visuals that used to sit over platform A end up over another platform, so every platform
+# has to re-identify "the set that is above it right now" — the line in `_mp8_wire_platform()`
+# `p.compactor = own_compactor` does exactly that, **and it uses each machine's own orientation**.
+#
+#   1.2 = view setting a, **2 orientations**, using vanilla's built-in two-state toggle (`compactor` ↔ `_flipped`),
+#         so server and clients always agree on "which compactor belongs to which platform" → it can't happen.
+#   1.3 = view setting c (the user settled on it as the default on 2026-08-25), **8 orientations**,
+#         each of the 8 machines computes its own → **every machine has a different answer to "which compactor platform i should slam"**.
+#         Platform collapse goes through `collapse_rpc` (runs on every peer), so each machine slams its own compactor,
+#         and the kill zone that comes down covers **different players** on different machines.
+#         Measured log — the two machines had orientations 225° and 270°, and each one "re-identified 8/8 platforms".
+#
+# **The visuals must rotate with the client's orientation (otherwise the picture is misaligned, and view C depends on it). Hit detection must never rotate.**
+# 1.3 rotated both roles together — vanilla left the fuse, and we're the ones who wired it up.
+#
+# ---- Once hit detection belongs to the server ----
+#
+# The client's Area still triggers as usual, it just no longer acts on it. Kills are still broadcast to every peer via `kill_rpc.rpc()`,
+# so it looks exactly the same. This matches the fall path, whose `fall_rpc` tween callback is guarded by `if multiplayer.is_server():`.
+#
+# ⚠️ This change does **not** fix "the same player is judged dead twice" — the duplicates still showed up in testing after the change.
+#    That is a separate issue (the same compactor's kill zone gets entered repeatedly), and the `body.active` guard below blocks it.
 func on_body_entered(body):
 	if body is JunkPlatformPlayer:
+
+		# [MP8] Only the server decides eliminations. Clients stop here — their compactor follows their own orientation,
+		# so the player it covers may well not be the one the server sees.
+		if not multiplayer.is_server():
+			return
+
+		# [MP8] Each life is only judged once.
+		# In testing (the match right after the compactor binding was fixed on 2026-08-27), every player was still judged dead twice, 40~60ms apart —
+		# while `slam()` pulls `visual` down, the same body enters the kill zone → gets pushed out → enters again.
+		# `_on_player_died` has an `active_players.has()` guard, so the duplicate itself changes neither score nor placement,
+		# **but it would make anyone reading the logs later think they had hit a network race**. Blocking it at the source keeps the logs clean.
+		# `kill_rpc()` always sets active to false, so this check holds for both death paths.
+		if not body.active:
+			return
+
 		body.kill_rpc.rpc()
 		body.emit_died.emit(body.player_presence.network_id)
-		if multiplayer.is_server():
-			manager.junk_handler.remove_spawn_point(body.player_presence.network_id)
+		manager.junk_handler.remove_spawn_point(body.player_presence.network_id)
